From 7c1d12997c4c1cddcf0954d7762a562f1872e18d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivy Evans <ivy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:40:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Tools: an XPRESS-with-Huffman encoder, for building the
 banks off Windows

tools/build_native_assets.py compresses the embedded banks through the Windows
Compression API (cabinet.dll, COMPRESS_ALGORITHM_XPRESS_HUFF), which exists only
on Windows. This is that encoding without the API, in exactly the form
K1Xpress.cpp decodes: the 24-byte container header, then per 65,536-byte chunk
its stored size and bytes, a chunk stored whole where coding would not shrink
it; inside a chunk the 256-byte table of 512 four-bit code lengths, then the
codes most significant bit first in 16-bit little-endian words, with one zero
word after the last for the decoder's two words of lookahead.

Matches are 3 to 17 bytes, so a length never spills into the byte stream and no
length byte is ever interleaved with the code words. Canonical codes are
assigned in the order BuildTable fills its table (rising length, then rising
symbol); a distribution whose longest code would pass 15 bits is flattened
until it fits.

Round-tripped through K1Xpress.cpp compiled natively on seven inputs (an empty
run of 65,536 zeros, 70,000 random bytes, one byte, a 23-byte string, a vanilla
.gui, repeated text, a controller texture): every one decoded to its input.
Ratios on the compressible ones are within a few percent of zlib's.
